\* Not in any of them -- separate packages: `sed`, `gawk`, `less`, `diffutils`, `vim`.

Reading off this:

**busybox is still the widest.** 304 applets in a 906 KiB package, covering what
coreutils, util-linux and procps-ng cover together, plus `sed`, `awk`, `vi`,
`tar`, `wget` and an init. Dropping it is not swapping one package for another;
it is giving up a userland and adding back only what you name.

**u-root is the closest thing to a busybox in Go** -- 117 commands, and the only
other column here that spans files, text, process and boot tooling at once. It
is aimed at initramfs and firmware rather than containers, and the gaps show:
no `chown`, no `stat`, no `sed` or `awk`, no `top`, no `su`/`login`.

**gokrazy is a zero, and that is the point.** Its `cmd/` holds `dhcp`,
`heartbeat`, `ntp` and `randomd` -- daemons, not commands. There is no shell and
no userland, because your Go programs *are* the appliance. Debugging access is
an add-on: `gokrazy/breakglass` for SSH, or `gokrazy/serial-busybox`, which
bundles a statically compiled busybox. It is not a smaller busybox; it is the
opposite approach.

**The two coreutils are not the same set.** uutils ships `kill`, `hostname`,
`more`, `uptime`, `groups` and `arch`, which GNU coreutils does not -- on a GNU
system those come from util-linux, procps-ng or inetutils.

**util-linux and procps-ng barely touch the first two tables.** util-linux owns
block devices, mounts, terminals and login; procps-ng owns the process table
(`ps`, `top`, `free`, `uptime`) in 17 commands. Installing util-linux on Alpine
pulls `busybox` and `busybox-binsh` in as dependencies, which is worth knowing
if avoiding busybox was the point.

## Coreutils
`UTILS=uutils` adds `uutils`: the Rust coreutils, built by this repo's CI
from `uutils/apkbuild/APKBUILD` as a single multicall binary holding only
the utilities we actually use, with a symlink per utility. Alpine's
`uutils-coreutils` compiles in all ~100; this one compiles in what is listed:

```sh
_utils="
	cat
	chmod
	...
	"
```

Adding one is a single line, a `pkgrel` bump and a rebuild. Anything in
upstream's `feat_os_unix_musl` set is safe; `stdbuf` is not -- it needs a
cdylib, which musl targets do not support.

```sh
make base UTILS=uutils   # base image with the selected utilities
```

## Build
```sh
make              # Every image
make test         # Run tests
make sizes        # Examine sizes
make shell        # brush in the base image
make brush-shell  # brush in a libc-free image
make sway-run     # start sway headless and print what it brought up
```

